# Attempt 1: Omarchy — Review

I liked Omarchy a lot. The selection of themes I could switch between with a
single click was the main reason I picked it in the first place.

It was also my first introduction to Hyprland, a very clean window manager. I
genuinely liked how clean it made the screen look: having multiple apps open
and watching them split into a neat tiled layout never got old for me.
Although there were a few issues I faced because of my NVIDIA graphics card,
which did take away from some of the experience, I really liked Hyprland
overall, and would use it again if it gets better NVIDIA support, or if I get
a new system that runs it more smoothly.

Omarchy was also a good gateway into the Arch side of Linux, which has a
reputation for being difficult to install. Omarchy isn't. It's genuinely easy
to get running, even as someone newer to Linux. It's also well documented,
like most Linux distros are, so if you get stuck, checking the documentation
usually helps. Since this was my first attempt at installing Linux at all,
Omarchy was one of the best distros to start with as a beginner. That said,
it was a bit overwhelming at times.

It also came pre-installed with a lot of popular software, like Chrome and
Firefox, which gets rid of the whole setup process that I've heard a lot of
people dislike.

Something else I really liked was the shortcut integration. It was
overwhelming at first, but the more I used it, the more I liked it. Need the
terminal? Press Super and Enter, and it pops up. I really liked that idea,
and the search bar when you press Super was exactly the kind of thing I
liked too.

## Would I use it again?

I'm planning on installing Omarchy again on its own system at some point,
since I really liked it. It's just a bit overwhelming as a beginner, so I'll
wait until I'm more comfortable with Linux in general.

I'd recommend it to people who want something quick to set up and get into.
I wouldn't recommend it to beginners, though, since like me, they might find
it overwhelming.

## Rating

**8.5/10**
